Q: Slow performance with SQL Server MERGE using Spring JdbcTemplate batchUpdate for ~14k records A: <p>Thanks to the commenters, I realized that using JdbcTemplate.batchUpdate with a MERGE statement falls back to RBAR (row-by-row) execution. The JDBC driver doesn't translate complex MERGE statements into true bulk operations like it does for simple INSERTs, resulting in ~22,000 separate db operations.</p>
<p>The Solution: OPENJSON
While I could squeeze out even more performance using more robust structures like Table-Valued Parameters (TVPs) or Temp Tables, passing the data as a single JSON parameter via OPENJSON was the perfect middle ground. It avoids DDL changes and keeps the Java code extremely clean.</p>
<ol>
<li>The SQL:</li>
</ol>
<p>SQL</p>
<pre><code>private static final String UPSERT_JSON_SQL = &quot;&quot;&quot;
MERGE INTO fund_return AS t
USING (
SELECT \* FROM OPENJSON(?) WITH (
fundCode varchar(8) '$.fundCode', currency varchar(3) '$.currency',
dataDate date '$.dataDate', period varchar(16) '$.period',
holidayType varchar(32) '$.holidayType', fundReturn numeric(30,15) '$.fundReturn',
depositReturnAnn numeric(30,15) '$.depositReturnAnn', fundBenchmarkReturn numeric(30,15) '$.fundBenchmarkReturn'
)
) AS s
ON t.fund_code = s.fundCode AND t.currency = s.currency
AND t.data_date = s.dataDate AND t.period = s.period AND t.holiday_type = s.holidayType
WHEN MATCHED THEN UPDATE SET
fund_return = s.fundReturn, deposit_return_ann = s.depositReturnAnn,
fund_benchmark_return = s.fundBenchmarkReturn, modif_time = GETDATE()
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN INSERT
(fund_code, currency, data_date, period, holiday_type, fund_return, deposit_return_ann, fund_benchmark_return, modif_time)
VALUES (s.fundCode, s.currency, s.dataDate, s.period, s.holidayType, s.fundReturn, s.depositReturnAnn, s.fundBenchmarkReturn, GETDATE());
&quot;&quot;&quot;;
</code></pre>
<p>2. The Java Code:</p>
<p>Java</p>
<pre><code>@Transactional
public void batchUpsert(List&lt;FundReturn&gt; returns) {
    if (returns == null || returns.isEmpty()) return;
    try {
        String jsonPayload = objectMapper.writeValueAsString(returns);
        jdbcTemplate.update(UPSERT_JSON_SQL, jsonPayload);
    } catch (Exception e) {
        throw new RuntimeException(&quot;Bulk Upsert failed&quot;, e);
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>The Result:
Execution time for ~22,000 records dropped from ~100 seconds to just 1-2 seconds. While TVP or Temp Tables could theoretically be faster for massive datasets, this execution time is perfectly sufficient for my scale and the implementation was virtually effortless.</p>

Q: How to re-render component after fetch request and state change? A: <p>You are probably not changing your state "todos" that is why it doesn't render. You could fetch todos after every change (after remove, update, add...) or change the state yourself.</p>

<p>Methode 1:</p>

<pre><code>componentDidMount() {
    this.getTodos();
}

getTodos = () =&gt; {
    //fetch todos, setState
}

handleSubmit = () =&gt; {
    fetch(...).then(this.getTodos);
}

handleDelete = () =&gt; {
    fetch(...).then(this.getTodos);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Methode 2:</p>

<pre><code>componentDidMount() {
    this.getTodos();
}

getTodos = () =&gt; {
